virglrenderer: shader constants through uniform buffers, one upload per submit

What: CONST[0] (a guest shader's plain uniforms, sent with SET_CONSTANT_BUFFER) becomes
the uniform block <prefix>constblk { uvec4 <prefix>const0[n]; } instead of the uniform
array <prefix>const0[n]. Before a submit is decoded, the payloads of its
SET_CONSTANT_BUFFER commands are copied into one staging block (vrend_const_prescan,
vrend_decode.c calls it; the bytes between them are zeroed); the first draw that needs
any of them uploads the block with one glBufferData into the next of four buffers of the
sub context. A vertex shader reads its constants through a window: its block holds
GL_MAX_UNIFORM_BLOCK_SIZE bytes, its references become <prefix>const0[virgl_cbase + i],
and virgl_cbase is an int vertex attribute at the last location (glBindAttribLocation,
also before the early link of a separable program; never an array), so a draw sets one
attribute value (glVertexAttribI1i) and binds the window only when its constants lie
outside the bound one. The upload is then padded by a window of zeros. The shader can
read only its own constants of the window: an index from ADDR is clamped to them, a
fixed index outside them is refused at compile time as uniforms refuse it. Other stages,
and vertex shaders that use every attribute location, bind their range per draw with
glBindBufferRange (as vrend already does for the VirglBlock). Constants that did not come
through the upload (another sub context in the same submit, a ring buffer that took a
later submit, an earlier submit, fewer constants than the shader reads, a range the GL
would not take at that offset) go into the stage's own buffer, zero-padded, on the draw
that needs them. Only where the block fits: desktop GL with uniform buffers, a context
with GLSL 1.40, not compute, at most GL_MAX_UNIFORM_BLOCK_SIZE, and room for this block
and the VirglBlock in the stage's uniform block limit. A program whose blocks then need
more bindings than the GL has (or whose virgl_cbase is not where it must be) has its
shaders built again with uniforms (shader key const_uniforms) and the draw selects again
at once. A program whose blocks reach the window's binding makes the next window draw
bind it again.
OMACVM_VIRGL_CONST_UBO=0 keeps every shader on uniforms (the code path from before),
=1 binds a range per draw in vertex shaders too; qemu.log says which at start.
OMACVM_VIRGL_CACHE_STATS=1 logs uploads, binds, index draws and window binds.
Why: Apple's GL redoes much of its draw setup after any glUniform call, whatever its
size (~0.53 us per draw on an M4 Max), and WebGL pages change uniforms before nearly
every draw (Aquarium 30k: ~31,000 SET_CONSTANT_BUFFER + draws a frame, QEMU's render
thread at 98 %). In the host-only measurement (bench/aquarium-fusion/drawcost-const.c)
a range bound per draw cost 0.19 us less per draw, a window plus an index attribute
0.35 us less. Aquarium 30k on an M4 Max (MacBook Pro): 32.5 -> 42.5 fps.
Upstream: not sent (depends on OmacVM's uniform block checks).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-const-ubo.c (soft GL + gl-oracle; per-draw constants in each
stage, stale ring buffers, short constants, an index in the shader, program and sub
context switches, a guest uniform buffer in the same shader, a cut-off command, a window
that moves, a vertex shader without a free location, a fixed index far past the
constants, separable programs, an upload holding nothing of an earlier submit; run in
all three modes), Tests/virgl/index-range-writes.py (the new buffer writes are vrend's
own).

